  {
    "label": "figure_title",
    "text": "FIG. 2: Exact stationary solution for tree-size distribu- tion shows the signature of the underlying dynamical regime and the local resource availability as a dual power-law shape. A: In the spatial mean-field case, the stationary solution of eq. (10), ρ*(h), as given in eq. (27), dis- plays a dual power-law shape separated by a crossover height hc. At lower tree sizes, the resource competition term domi- nates. In this size range, the distribution follows a power-law decrease with exponent −a, where a = R*/Ra is the resource consumption ratio. Above the crossover size hc, the spatial competition term dominates and the power-law exponent be- comes -(1 + 2H), which is linked only to the tree allometric scaling relation. Parameters: κ = 0.001 and Ra = 220 (imply- ing ρ*(h0) ≈ 0.11, a≈ 0.5, and hc ≈ 4.2). B: If the available resources are very abundant and / or the sampling recruitment rate is high, the crossover point shifts towards very low values hc → h0. In this scenario only the power law with exponent −(1 + 2H) is visible. Parameters: κ = 10 and Ra = 235 (ρ*(h0) ≈ 1.2 · 103, a≈0.5, and hc ≈0.1). C: If the available resources are scarce or seed injection / recruitment is low, the crossover point shifts to high values, hc → hu. In this case, the only visible power law has exponent —a, with a < 1 + 2H. Parameters: κ = 10−6 and Ra = 80 (implying ρ*(h0)≈4·10−5, a≈0.5, and hc≈101). Common parameters across all panels: H = 1, h0 = hb = 0.1, γ = 1, hu = 100.",
